    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The M7.3 June 28 1992 Landers earthquake ruptured N and NW across segments of five sub- parallel faults. The rupture geometry in plan-view is complex and raises the question as to whether the fault structure at depth is equally complex. This project aims to constrain the subsurface fault geometry by comparing aftershock focal mechanisms with modeled failure planes determined at aftershock locations. Observed aftershocks are taken as evidence of the local stress state at depth and models are evaluated by their ability to reproduce that local stress. Structural models with variable levels of fault segmentation, remote stress orientation, and fault dip are compared. The local stress state at depth likely is influenced by the subsurface geology, which controls both in the internal angle of friction and the elastic moduli. The effect of lateral and vertical changes in geology are not modeled, but are addressed in the final section.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
